## §2 Authoritative Sources

| Source ID | Reference | Version | Date Verified |
|-----------|-----------|---------|---------------|
| EXAMPLE-1 | [Full source name with link if public](https://example.org) | X.Y | YYYY-MM-DD |
| EXAMPLE-2 | NIST SP 800-NNN | Rev N | YYYY-MM-DD |
| EXAMPLE-3 | OWASP ASVS 5.0 Chapter X | 5.0 | YYYY-MM-DD |
| EXAMPLE-4 | ISO/IEC 27002:2022 control X.Y | 2022 | YYYY-MM-DD |

Sources MUST be authoritative — official standards, NIST/OWASP/ISO/MITRE publications, RFCs, vendor primary documentation. Blog posts, Stack Overflow, and Medium articles are NOT acceptable as primary sources.

**Citation granularity** per Phase 4 Checkpoint 1 Decision A: cite at the source's natural granularity. For sources with sub-rule numbering (OWASP ASVS V6.2.2, NIST SP 800-63B §5.1.1.2), use the sub-rule identifier. For sources where practices ARE the granular level (NIST SSDF practices like PW.4), cite the practice. For craft rules with no rule-level mapping in any authoritative source, cite the closest principle-level source and acknowledge the rule as TGF synthesis of senior-engineer craft.

Source IDs are referenced throughout the rule summaries and anti-pattern summaries to maintain the citation chain.
